C++ Collection of Polymorphic Shape Pointers
Description

This C++ program demonstrates run-time polymorphism by storing different shape objects through pointers to a common abstract Shape class.

The program uses vector and unique_ptr to store Rectangle and Circle objects. Each object provides its own implementation of area() and displayName().

Classes Used
1. Shape Class

The Shape class is the abstract base class.

It contains the pure virtual area() function.
It contains the pure virtual displayName() function.
It has a virtual destructor.
It provides a common interface for different shapes.

The functions are:

virtual double area() const = 0;
virtual void displayName() const = 0;
2. Rectangle Class

The Rectangle class is derived from Shape.

length stores the length.
width stores the width.
area() calculates the rectangle's area.
displayName() displays "Rectangle".

Formula:

Area = Length × Width
3. Circle Class

The Circle class is also derived from Shape.

radius stores the radius.
area() calculates the circle's area.
displayName() displays "Circle".

Formula:

Area = π × Radius × Radius
Main Function

In the main() function, a vector is created to store pointers to Shape objects:

vector<unique_ptr<Shape>> shapes;

Two objects are then added:

Rectangle → length 5.0, width 3.0
Circle → radius 2.0

The for loop processes every shape and calls:

shape->displayName();
shape->area();

Because these functions are virtual, the appropriate derived-class implementation is called.

Output
Rectangle Area: 15
Circle Area: 12.5664
OOP Concept Used

Run-Time Polymorphism — Collection of Polymorphic Objects

                  Shape
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
      Rectangle             Circle
          |                   |
       Area = 15          Area = 12.5664
          \                   /
           \                 /
            vector<unique_ptr<Shape>>

The program demonstrates how different derived objects can be processed through a common abstract base-class interface. unique_ptr also provides safe ownership of the dynamically managed objects.